plan Artifacts
title   Persistence and artifact evidence
status  archived
goal
    Define one Flow-owned boundary for persistence, capture, codecs, inspection, and Story evidence.
    Include canonical private artifacts and export-only redaction.
    This Plan owns migrated semantics under @plan.Verification.D1. The source contract
    pack supplies provenance and trace IDs; ARTIFACT_WIRE.md is a pointer only.
    CONTRACT.md §§7 and 9 provide unaccepted examples. Implementation and product
    cutover remain pending under @plan.Cutover.O3 and @plan.Cutover.V7.
    Operation identity belongs to @plan.Operations.O2 and @plan.Operations.O3.
    Runtime and host integration belong to @plan.Runtime.O3, @plan.Runtime.O5, and @plan.Hosts.O4.
    CLI projection belongs to @plan.CLI.O2 and @plan.CLI.O3.
outcomes
    O1  Persistence is optional, explicit, inert at construction, and Runtime-owned
        - Persistence is an optional, inert RuntimeSetup provider.
        - It requires storage and scope; codec and identity-only filter are optional and pure.
        - No provider means no persistence I/O.
        - `RuntimeSetup.construct()` stays synchronous and inert.
        - It may retain app identity, Clock, external capabilities, and the provider.
        - It does not acquire Implementation, create/register actors, start work, or expose handles.
        - The provider is not an Implementation, AppPlan input, second Runtime, or cross-tab
          synchronizer.
        - It is not a conflict resolver or same-scope writer protocol.
        - It represents one logical record attached to one Runtime and scope.
        - Cross-tab and concurrent same-scope coordination are undefined.
        - Fixtures and `story.machine` do not inherit browser/session storage by default.

        - The illustrative setup is:

        ```ts
        const setup = runtimeSetup({
          app: TodoApp,
          implementation: TodoLive,
          persistence: persistence({
            storage: webStorage(window.localStorage),
            scope: "user:42",
            codec: todoPersistenceCodec,
            filter: ({ kind, id }) => kind !== "stream" || id === "todo.updates",
          }),
        });
        ```

        - The public notation is bounded by these shapes:

        ```ts
        type PersistenceStorage = {
          read(): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array | undefined, PersistenceStorageError>;
          write(value: Uint8Array): Effect.Effect<void, PersistenceStorageError>;
          remove(): Effect.Effect<void, PersistenceStorageError>;
        };

        type Persistence = {
          storage: PersistenceStorage;
          scope: string;
          codec: PersistenceCodec;
          filter?: (entry: PersistenceEntry) => boolean;
        };

        declare function webStorage(storage: Storage): PersistenceStorage;
        declare function indexedDbStorage(storage: IndexedDBStorage): PersistenceStorage;

        type PersistenceCodec = {
          encode(value: unknown, slot: PersistenceSlot): PersistenceValue;
          decode(value: PersistenceValue, slot: PersistenceSlot): unknown;
        };

        declare function persistence(options: {
          storage: PersistenceStorage;
          scope: string;
          codec?: PersistenceCodec;
          filter?: (entry: PersistenceEntry) => boolean;
        }): Persistence;
        ```

        - PersistenceStorage has typed read, write, and remove Effects.
        - Web Storage and IndexedDB adapters share typed failure, cancellation, and lifetime
          boundaries.
        - PersistenceCodec is synchronous and pure at the value boundary.
        - It never accesses storage, creates actors, runs Effects, resumes work, or decodes a full
          boot payload.
        - PersistenceValue is bounded canonical JSON.
        - PersistenceSlot identifies actor, resource, transaction, or stream slots.
        - PersistenceEntry exposes only stable identity metadata and canonical K.
        - The default codec rejects unsupported values instead of stringifying or losing data.
        - Flow owns the envelope, app identity, persistenceVersion, plan compatibility, bounds, and
          executable-work rejection.

        - Stable actor refs and resource, transaction, and stream declarations each require
          `persist: true`.
        - The default is false; durable operations never make their actor durable implicitly.
        - A filter sees kind, stable identity, owning stable actor, and canonical K only.
        - It can exclude declared entries but cannot select undeclared data.
        - It cannot inspect executable P, mutable actor state, services, operation results, or
          storage
          ownership.

        - Runtime owns restore, observation, ordered writes, coalescing, and cleanup.
        - Readiness validates and restores once, then installs the canonical store.
        - It restores providers before consumers and completes bootstrap before handles or external
          work escape.
        - Runtime then observes committed publications through the shared DehydrateBarrier.
        - Writes serialize per scope; older writes cannot replace newer ones.
        - Disposal stops observation, finalizes the latest accepted write, then releases the
          provider.
        - A write failure preserves Runtime state and the last successful record.
        - It remains the provider's write failure; it does not fail an actor turn after publication.
        - Storage, codec, identity/version, malformed-data, concurrent-capture, and
          non-durable-context-provider failures are internal `Diagnostic` categories.
        - They map to `StorageFailure`, `CodecFailure`, `IdentityVersionMismatch`,
          `MalformedData`, `ConcurrentCapture`, and `NonDurableContextProvider` codes.
        - `FlowPersistenceError` is only a terminal host compatibility envelope when an existing
          JavaScript boundary requires that name; no stable fields or message are specified.
        - Raw Effect Cause may be retained only at readiness/disposal boundaries.


    O2  Durable identity, app-compatible boot, and provider-owned actor state are exact
        - P is the complete executable input retained by a live binding or generation.
        - K is the ordered, synchronous result of `key(P)`.
        - Descriptor ID plus K defines identity.
        - P is never reconstructed from K; K never substitutes for capabilities or authorizes
          execution/lookup.
        - Flow validates structure; the application codec validates opaque domain values.
        - Compatibility is checked against the receiving compiled AppPlan.
        - No generic identity migration exists.
        - Application codecs are synchronous and pure.
        - They produce data accepted by the receiving app and never encode executable work.

        - Keys accept dense arrays and plain records copied into Flow-owned containers.
        - Record keys sort; `-0` normalizes to numeric zero before the general artifact walker.
        - KBytes use exact UTF-8 from the portable boundary.
        - Include capability, tenant, account, network, and session identity discriminators when
          relevant.
        - Key validation is limited to 16 nested levels, 256 value nodes, and 8 KiB KBytes.
        - Canonical encoding is synchronous and stable.
        - Own the encoded key before admission or external work.
        - Keep structural and application-domain failures distinct.
        - Secrets remain observable unless the application hashes/replaces them before key
          projection.
        - Reject hostile reflection, unsupported values, and assertion-cast persisted input.
        - Reject aliased durable machine, descriptor, state, event, and actor identities.
        - Reject actor-private canonical resource caches and serialized executable work.

        - Example declarations:

        ```ts
        const todos = resource({
          id: "todo.todos",
          key: ({ listId }: { listId: string }) => [listId],
          lookup: loadTodos,
          persist: true,
        });
        const Primary = actorRef(TodoMachine, "primary", { persist: true });
        ```

        - Provider bytes are runtime input. Readiness follows one ordered boot:
          1. Validate the record, select the default/provider codec, and prepare detached data.
             This step installs no actors or store state.
          2. Acquire Implementation services.
          3. Install prepared actor state, operation facts, and the canonical resource store.
          4. Complete initial ensureActor calls and resolve exact context refs.
          5. Seal the graph, then activate actors and expose handles.
        - Boot retains app ID, application-owned persistenceVersion, and durable identity facts
          resolved through the receiving AppPlan.
        - Boot accepts prepared materialized memory, restored committed bases, generation fencing,
          overlay provenance, stale-completion facts, and actor observed-store revisions.
        - Preserve exact contextBindings refs and provider revisions.
        - Install hydrated state without fresh-memory initialization.
        - Install a silent provider-closed context baseline before continuing-activity
          reconciliation or handle escape.
        - Failed or incomplete bootstrap exposes no actor, external work, or handle.

        - Stable actors use authored machine-branded `actorRef(machine, id, { persist: true })`.
        - `runtime.ensureActor(ref, ...)` owns restore-or-create.
        - `runtime.createActor(machine, ...)` is ID-free and always creates a fresh opaque local
          actor.
        - A stable actor retains its exact ref and machine identity.
        - It also retains state, memory, durable bindings, operation facts, issues, and observed
          revisions.
        - It may retain exact referenced refs and its last observed store revision.
        - Private state may retain observe revisions, finite-binding cursors, timer due facts,
          consumed activation facts, and pending mapped outcomes.
        - It never serializes queue cells or commands.
        - Stable wire identity uses the actor namespace and length-prefixed machine and authored-ID
          segments defined by @plan.Runtime.O1; durable identity belongs to @plan.Runtime.O2.
        - Prefix and bytes use the shared portable UTF-8 boundary.
        - Never use Node Buffer or UTF-16 string length.

        - Capture only registered, non-disposed stable actors explicitly marked durable.
        - Include suspended actors and the transitive exact context-provider closure.
        - Exclude opaque refs, runtime-local actors, tombstones, and disposed actors.
        - Exclude automatic/dynamic/child actors and subordinate-machine actors.
        - Operation declarations never imply actor durability.
        - Never serialize mailboxes, queues, fibers, Scopes, callbacks, services, cursors,
          transports,
          buffered emissions, pending commands, or executable work.
        - Running stream persistence retains declaration, key, generation, ownership, and latest
          projection only.
        - A captured stream waits for pending outcomes to drain.
        - It then hydrates from live P in a new generation.
        - Terminal streams do not restart; missing P fails closed.
        - A durable consumer of an opaque provider fails `NonDurableContextProvider`.
        - Flow never promotes or substitutes that provider.
        - A restored stable actor remains Runtime-owned and captureable without repeated
          `ensureActor`.
        - Deleted automatic/dynamic roots, parent-child, and subordinate-machine persistence have no
          aliases.

        - Operation identity and settlement are shared with @plan.Operations.O2 and
          @plan.Operations.O3. This outcome owns their durable facts and restore normalization.

    O3  Capture and hydration form a consistent, context-closed cut without replay
        - Capture shares the StoreKernel commit window:
          1. Lease actor selection.
          2. Capture StoreState once.
          3. Read each actor atomically.
          4. Require each observedStoreRevision to be no newer than capturedStoreRevision.
        - Start only between completed context-propagation waves.
        - Use the exact static durable actor/context closure. Unrelated actors may be at different
          state-only instants.
        - Published snapshots and suspended stable actors are valid. Each binding preserves its
          exact observed provider revision.
        - Reject mixed store cuts and newer actor revisions.
        - Reject concurrent closure changes, orphan payloads, and partial payloads.
        - Reject current-projection membership and runtime-local actors.
        - Reject serialized selected-context overrides, subordinate replay, and duplicated
          actor-owned values.
        - A concurrent mismatch is retryable ConcurrentDehydrate.

        - Capture membership uses declaration metadata, Runtime registration, and fixed bindings.
        - It does not use projection or a repeated `ensureActor` call.
        - An included durable consumer with a direct/transitive opaque provider fails terminally
          with
          `NonDurableContextProvider`.
        - Do not serialize, promote, recreate, substitute, or rebind that provider.
        - A selected durable consumer whose required stable provider has persist false, or is
          excluded by persistence filtering, rejects the whole selected capture closure with
          NonDurableContextProvider. Never promote the provider or silently omit consumers.
        - Capture never performs external work.

        - Hydration follows this order:
          1. Install the canonical store.
          2. Rebuild resource and transaction projections from exact refs and StoreState.
          3. Restore providers before consumers in dependency order.
          4. Evaluate selectors from restored providers.
          5. Install derived context as the silent baseline.
          6. Reconcile continuing activity before handle escape.
        - Do not manufacture onContext events, execute external work, replay finite work, or use a
          second source of selected-context truth.

        - Resource in-flight execution does not resume.
        - Key-only entries stay passive until a live binding supplies P.
        - Running equal-key generations retain pinned P.
        - Automatic generations use the oldest eligible live binding; explicit refetch uses caller
          P.
        - Running streams receive a new owned generation from live P without emission replay.
        - Pending transaction occurrences never replay; they become interrupted.
        - Durable remote identities become unknown/reconciliation-required.
        - Reconcile them with the same identity.
        - Terminal transactions restore only through the accepted production operation
          representation.
        - Restored resources follow @plan.PublicSurface.O6 collection and ownership rules.
        - Actor-effective resource reads apply only the owner's preview layers.
        - Add no replacement lookup policy, generic transaction retry, terminal-stream restart, or
          restored gcTime/default.
        - Hydration drains pending outcomes before rematerializing continuing declarations.
        - Missing executable input fails closed.


    O4  One bounded structural codec validates boot and artifacts with typed failures
        - Persistence restore, artifact import, and export share one Flow-owned validation path.
        - They produce the same package-private v2 decoded model.
        - Public bytes pass strict UTF-8, at most one supported compression member, and decompressed
          byte limits.
        - They then pass JSON parsing and one package-private Effect Schema decoder.
        - The decoder produces fresh package-owned canonical data.
        - Runtime readiness applies the same persisted-structure rules before actor creation or
          external work.
        - Flow validates versions, identities, descriptors, tokens, revisions, and bounds.
        - The application codec validates opaque values before Runtime construction.
        - Raw parser/library errors never escape public Flow APIs.
        - Public bytes are stable-key UTF-8 JSON or exactly one gzip member.
        - Compressed import stops when it crosses the decompressed-byte limit.

        - DiagnosticProjection is the only serialized projection of the typed Diagnostic model in
          @plan.Diagnostics.O1.
        - Diagnostic owns stable classification, code, path, and details.
        - It also owns evolving summary/help and safe projection/rendering.
        - Encoders may order and bound fields under the wire rules.
        - They must not invent another diagnostic type or mirror an Effect Cause tree.
        - DiagnosticCode is closed.
        - Failures retain structural, bound, identity, decompression, external-input, application,
          cleanup, interruption, I/O, or invariant ownership.
        - Bound diagnostics identify limit and path separately from other failure kinds.
        - No failure becomes undefined or generic corrupt-artifact.

        - Its illustrative alias is:

        ```ts
        type NonNegative = number; // accepted only when Number.isSafeInteger(value) && value >= 0
        ```

        - Persisted/artifact counters, revisions, generations, sequences, and timestamps are
          non-negative safe integers.
        - A boot counter at `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` is invalid.
        - Terminal-publication and Runtime-cleanup credits must remain.
        - Portable canonical encoding/order uses host-neutral TextEncoder.
        - Runtime encoding rejects lone surrogates.
        - Shared package code never uses Buffer.
        - Compile-time UTF-8 lengths are never approximated.

        - Bounds apply uniformly to boot, trace, behavior, and opaque carriers.
        - No envelope overrides these bounds.
        - Depth is 32, with root at zero; visit at most 10,000 scalar/container nodes.
        - Arrays contain at most 4,096 items.
        - Each string/property key is at most 262,144 UTF-8 bytes.
        - Decompressed/canonical UTF-8 is at most 2,097,152 bytes.
        - Reject parser errors, malformed/cyclic data, serialized negative zero, and non-finite
          values.
        - Reject detectable duplicate keys, concatenated gzip members, trailing bytes, unsupported
          compression, and over-limit input.
        - Require duplicate-key detection where the parser can provide it.
        - Direct private-decoder behavior is unspecified for proxies, getters, custom prototypes,
          descriptors, symbol properties, sparse arrays, or concurrent mutation.
        - Raw Effect Cause never enters a carrier.


    O5  Inspection evidence is globally ordered, bounded, and separate from persistence
        - Each successful turn exposes one atomic committed actor/store cut.
        - Accepted evidence uses one Runtime-global sequence and represents committed cuts only.
        - Evidence remains globally ordered.
        - Story acknowledgment follows actor/store publication and evidence acceptance.
        - It never waits for user Effects.
        - StoreFanout preserves causal order with the initiating publication.
        - Lifecycle records are inspection evidence, never machine turns.
        - Runtime observation is not persistence.
        - Records become durable only through explicit export from a configured sink.
        - Export preserves order, truncation marker, app identity, and format version.

        - A sink attaches once to one Runtime.
        - It receives records admitted after attachment.
        - Its buffer exposes `snapshot(): { records: InspectionRecord[]; truncatedBeforeSequence:
          number | null }` and `clear(): void`.
        - Capacity is explicit and bounded, defaults to 256, and must be a non-negative integer.
        - Capacity zero retains no records while preserving truncation.
        - A fresh snapshot starts at publication revision zero.
        - Hydration restores persisted publication and machine-turn revisions.
        - A pre-attachment snapshot is empty with a null marker.
        - Late attachment marks the greatest unseen global sequence, unless current sequence is
          zero.
        - One sink cannot attach to multiple Runtimes.

        - The truncation marker is null exactly when no accepted evidence was omitted.
        - Otherwise it is the greatest omitted or explicitly cleared global sequence.
        - Clear does not reset sequence; it advances only through the retained tail.
        - Already-accepted queued records may arrive after clear.
        - Attachment drain/dispose waits only through accepted prefixes before detaching.
        - A sink callback failure is isolated to that attachment.
        - Sinks never silently drop records, share mixed Runtime state, or retain actor-owned
          history.
        - They never delay acknowledgments/StoreFanout, roll back truth, mutate commits, or persist
          inspection buffers.
        - Permit/reservation/queue/gate/attachment/drain algorithms remain implementation details.
        - After commit acceptance, receipt, inspection, trace, CLI, and optional artifact sinks may
          process the record.
        - Actor admission and ordinary commits preflight changing actor/store/sequence reserves for
          cleanup.

        - A StoreState TurnRecord may include exact changed refs for that revision.
        - Changed refs are revision-local hints.
        - If a consumer skips revisions, it rereads state instead of combining incomplete hints into
          a historical diff.
        - Actor start/restore/suspend/resume/dispose are lifecycle facts only.
        - They are never machine revisions or TurnRecords.
        - Runtime disposal keeps reverse-dependency order and never creates a terminal actor turn.
        - Trace proof requires complete evidence and rejects every non-null truncation marker.
        - An @O6 decoder rejects a marker inconsistent with retained records.


        - API-016's raw export call form remains illustrative. @plan.PublicSurface.O6
          owns the accepted redaction option signature; AMEND-API-003 is its source trace.

        ```ts
        const attachment = attachInspectionSink(runtime, sink);
        await attachment.drain();
        const bytes = new Uint8Array(); // illustrative artifact input
        const trace = importTraceArtifact(bytes);
        const artifact = exportTraceArtifact(trace);
        ```

    O6  Exact package-private v2 artifacts and redacted share exports are canonical
        - Behavior and trace data use one exact package-private schema.
        - Story and CLI receive only that decoded model.
        - Do not serialize a public v2 model, second decoder, or duplicate nested authority.
        - Do not serialize raw Effect Cause, fixtures, callbacks, Effects, Runtime actors, live
          refs,
          or Runtime state.
        - Text/JSON output and file publication are projections, not schemas.
        - `ARTIFACT_WIRE.md` contributes no schema or notation.
        - @plan.Artifacts.O6 owns the persistence/artifact semantics.
        - Migration history records provenance only.
        - @plan.PublicSurface.O6 owns the public redaction signature; AMEND-API-003 and
          WIRE-020C are source trace IDs. @plan.Artifacts.O6 owns schema and behavior;
          @plan.Verification.V6 routes PROOF-014/API-P04.

        - A behavior artifact has kind `behavior-contract` and version
          `flow-state/behavior-contract.v2`.
        - It contains appId, persistenceVersion, appPlanFingerprint, requirements, modules,
          machines,
          and app/machine Story metadata.
        - It carries declaration and coverage identity only.
        - A trace has kind `trace-artifact` and version `flow-state/trace-artifact.v2`.
        - It contains storyId, appId, persistenceVersion, appPlanFingerprint, capturedAt,
          truncation, ordered TurnRecord/LifecycleRecord projections, checkpoints, outcome, end,
          failure, and cleanup.
        - App Story checkpoints may target `checkpoint.actor(storyActor | actorRef)`.
        - They include reserved Runtime metadata.
        - Operation facts are closed resource/transaction/stream unions.
        - Context, store, timer, and issue facts retain exact identities and order.

        - Resource facts are missing, pending, ready, refreshing, failure, defect, or interrupted.
        - Transaction facts are idle, pending, success, failure, defect, interrupted, or unknown.
        - Unknown transactions set `reconcileRequired: true`.
        - Stream facts are idle, running, complete, interrupted, failure, or defect.
        - Stream facts preserve the exact `hasValue`/latest/emissionCount union.
        - Context, store, timer, and issue facts retain their closed fields.
        - Missing/idle operation lanes use null generation and occurrence where specified.
        - Inconsistent generation, occurrence, retention, latest, or reconciliation fields reject as
          `InvalidTraceRecord` before application or Runtime acquisition.

        - Reject callbacks, Effects, fixtures, live Runtime objects, and legacy final/children
          fields.
        - Reject Scenario/v1 shapes, unknown members, and omitted nullable fields.
        - Reject malformed operation discriminants, invalid lifecycle tuples, and duplicate
          checkpoint names.
        - Reject unresolved requirements, duplicate IDs, invalid state defaults/Story metadata, and
          mismatched appPlanFingerprint.
        - The normalized requirements table has one record per static requirement identity.
        - Each operation requirement ID resolves to exactly one record.
        - Every nullable field serializes as null.
        - Completed traces have non-null end, null failure, and complete cleanup.
        - Failed traces have failure and root cleanup status consistent with cleanup diagnostics.
        - `end` is named end, never final, and never means actor completion.
        - Invalid IDs map to InvalidDescriptorId; duplicate IDs and operation/slot IDs map to
          DuplicateDescriptorId.
        - Duplicate machine values map to DuplicateMachineValue; duplicate module ownership maps to
          DuplicateModuleOwnership.
        - Missing module refs map to MissingModuleReference; invalid defaults map to
          InvalidStateDefault.
        - Unresolved operation requirements map to UnresolvedRequirementId.
        - Invalid Story metadata maps to InvalidStoryMetadata.
        - Malformed trace records map to InvalidTraceRecord.
        - Impossible lifecycle combinations map to InvalidLifecycleTransition.
        - Inconsistent end/failure/cleanup maps to InvalidStoryEvidence.
        - Errors retain exact category, path, and bound details.
        - Never collapse errors to undefined or generic corruption.

        - Canonical JSON orders object keys and ID-indexed declarations by UTF-8 bytes.
        - It orders requirement lists the same way.
        - It preserves authored child/event/checkpoint/record/diagnostic/fact order.
        - It uses JSON.stringify for finite numbers and performs no Unicode normalization.
        - Add exactly one trailing newline at the file boundary.
        - Artifacts are uncompressed stable-key UTF-8 JSON.
        - The private lowercase-hex SHA-256 preimage is canonical
          `{ appId, persistenceVersion, requirements, modules, machines }`.
        - It excludes Stories and the trailing newline.
        - The package-private behavior builder owns the preimage.
        - The encoder recomputes and verifies the emitted fingerprint.
        - Import compares it to the receiving compiled AppPlan.
        - Reject mismatches as `ArtifactIdentityMismatch`.
        - No public fingerprint or collision-resolution API exists.

        - The @O6 export-only share projection has this exact declared shape:

        ```ts
        type ArtifactCanonicalPath = (string | number)[];
        type ArtifactValuePath =
          | ["records", number, "snapshot", "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath]
          | ["records", number, "facts", number, "data" | "value" | "error" | "defect" | "latest", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath]
          | ["checkpoints", number, "actors", number, "snapshot", "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath]
          | ["checkpoints", number, "snapshot", "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath];

        type TraceShareArtifactBase = {
          kind: "trace-share-artifact";
          version: "flow-state/trace-share-artifact.v2";
          storyId: StableId;
          appId: StableId;
          persistenceVersion: string;
          appPlanFingerprint: string;
          capturedAt: NonNegative;
          truncatedBeforeSequence: Nullable<NonNegative>;
          records: TraceRecord[];
          checkpoints: Checkpoint[];
          redactions: ArtifactValuePath[];
        };
        type TraceShareArtifact =
          | (TraceShareArtifactBase & { outcome: "completed"; end: Checkpoint; failure: null; cleanup: { status: "complete" } })
          | (TraceShareArtifactBase & { outcome: "failed"; end: Nullable<Checkpoint>; failure: StoryFailure; cleanup: { status: "complete" } | { status: "failed"; diagnostics: Diagnostic[] } });
        ```

        - A non-empty finite redaction policy starts from a validated @O6 trace.
        - Copy every base/outcome field without changing identity, fingerprint, or capture time.
        - Preserve truncation, record/checkpoint order, sequence, diagnostics, outcome, end,
          failure,
          and cleanup.
        - Change only kind, version, selected application values, and the required non-empty
          manifest.
        - Each exact caller tuple names an existing application-owned carrier.
        - Replace that carrier with the fixed `"[REDACTED]"` value.
        - An unmanifested naturally occurring replacement string stays legal.
        - Duplicate paths and ancestor/descendant overlaps reject as `InvalidArtifactOperand` before
          projection.
        - Manifest paths sort segment-wise, with numbers before strings.
        - Numbers sort numerically; strings sort by UTF-8 bytes without normalization.
        - A shorter equal-prefix path sorts first.
        - Numeric segments are non-negative safe indexes inside arrays; strings address object
          members.
        - Wildcards and string-form paths are forbidden.
        - Diagnostics may display `records[3].snapshot.context.tokens.secret[4].first`.
        - Non-identifier names use JSON-quoted brackets; display strings are never input.

        - Share output uses @O6 stable-key canonical JSON and all @O4 bounds, including manifest
          bounds.
        - `exportTraceArtifact` always emits it uncompressed.
        - Omitted or empty redactions bypass @O6 and return byte-identical @O6 bytes.
        - Share data has no importer/decoder, CLI input, replay, persistence, boot, evidence
          authority, or mutation path.
        - Missing paths, structural/identity targets, or protected-field/order changes reject.
        - `importTraceArtifact` checks top-level kind first.
        - It rejects share data as `WrongArtifactKind` before @O6 unknown-member, identity, or
          fingerprint validation.
        - This enables privacy-reduced sharing without making share data raw evidence or executable
          input.

        - `CliCommand` is exactly behavior.build, behavior.render, behavior.diff, behavior.check,
          story.list, story.describe, story.run, trace.summarize, trace.proof, or trace.diff.
        - Build/render/list/describe/story.run use completed.
        - Behavior/trace comparisons use equal/different.
        - Trace diff is incomplete when retained facts match but either trace is truncated.
        - Trace summarize is completed or incomplete; trace proof requires complete evidence.
        - Story-run execution/cancellation/cleanup/trace-write failures use `CliError`.
        - Partial Story failure stays primary; ordered diagnostics follow as secondary failures.
        - Serialized evidence uses ordered DiagnosticProjection only.
        - In-process FlowDisposeError/FlowStoryExecutionError retain full Effect Cause only after
          classification and cleanup aggregation.
        - Serialized data does not mirror Cause tree, traversal order, or fiber ordinals.
        - Preserve independent cleanup diagnostic order and multiplicity.
        - FlowStoryExecutionError carries completed checkpoints, optional end, failure boundary,
          primary diagnostic, ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancellation evidence, accepted/drained
          evidence-sequence facts, and full public Cause.
        - Cleanup failure retains a captured end but cannot produce successful Story evidence.
        - Failure before end capture never invents `run.end`.
        - The complete CliResult data union stays private and shared with CLI.


    O7  Story evidence is a truthful capture. Pure machine models stay non-executable
        - A named checkpoint captures one atomic published evidence cut.
        - It covers exact actor targets, Runtime pending work, and TestClock time.
        - App evidence resolves storyActor or ActorRef; machine evidence exposes its single
          snapshot.
        - Captures use the DehydrateBarrier, one store revision, published actor snapshots,
          TestClock,
          and accepted evidence prefix.
        - End evidence is named `run.end`; it is not actor finality.
        - A failed run retains completed checkpoints and truthful cleanup.
        - Cancellation closes admission, preserves partial evidence, and awaits non-abortable
          cleanup.
        - Cancellation then fails; success implies complete disposal.
        - Never fabricate end after end-capture failure or report success after cleanup failure.
        - FlowStoryExecutionError retains complete public Cause only in process.

        - Pure model artifacts accept only a command-empty, fresh story.machine plan and contain no
          Effect. Model discovery does no work processing/time movement/actor 
          creation/restoration/live
          lookup/external work. Candidates belong to the traversal call. Base Story remains command-
          empty. They do not replace app Story cross-actor orchestration or persist fixture-declared
          outcomes. Model traversal is not Runtime-backed and does not instantiate fixture
          Implementation, maintain hidden candidate registries/event sets, persist fixture outcomes,
          or own replay. Model artifacts never serialize/execute transaction Effects, stream
          programs,
          patch functions, fibers, Queues, Deferreds, or Scopes. A model path becomes an ordinary
          live
          Story on the production Runtime.


        - The public redaction signature (source trace AMEND-API-003) is:

        ```ts
        type ArtifactExportOptions = {
          redactions?: {
            path: ArtifactValuePath;
            replacement: "[REDACTED]";
          }[];
        };

        declare function exportTraceArtifact(
          trace: ReturnType<typeof importTraceArtifact>,
          options?: ArtifactExportOptions,
        ): Uint8Array;
        ```
decisions
    D1  Flow owns one schema and decoder
        - Persistence, behavior, trace, and share encodings use one canonical structural walker.
          They share bounded validation and one package-private decoded model.
          Application codecs handle opaque values. Adapters own I/O. CLI owns transport/projection.
          Diagnostic remains the typed failure authority. ARTIFACT_WIRE.md is a pointer only.
    D2  Share artifacts are export-only projections
        - A non-empty valid redaction policy produces the share projection from a validated trace.
          An omitted or empty policy returns byte-identical raw output.
          Share output never replaces raw evidence or becomes importable, replayable, or boot input.
          @plan.PublicSurface.O6 owns the signature; AMEND-API-003 is its source trace.
          @O6 owns schema and behavior. PROOF-014/API-P04 route through
          @plan.Verification.V6. CONTRACT.md §7 is provenance only.
constraints
    C1  Persistence is opt-in and cannot serialize executable runtime authority
        - Stable refs and operation declarations opt in separately. A filter cannot select
          undeclared data or access P. A codec cannot execute work or restore the whole boot
          payload.
          No provider I/O occurs at construction. No automatic durability, cross-tab behavior,
          conflict resolver, or same-scope writer protocol is implied.
    C2  Keep resource identity, finite work, and stream restoration distinct
        - Descriptor plus K is identity. It never replaces live P.
          Finite work is never replayed. Remote transaction identity is reconciled.
          Streams restart only from live P in a fresh generation. Terminal streams do not restart.
          Do not invent lookup, collection, or gcTime policy. See @plan.PublicSurface.O6.
    C3  Keep schema and canonical bounds closed
        - Add no members, aliases, migration envelopes, alternate decoders, or Cause trees.
          Add no per-envelope limit overrides. Preserve nullable fields, order, and code mappings.
          @O4 and @O6 own structural limits and exact schema behavior.
    C4  Keep proposal status and unspecified API forms visible
        - Workflow Plans define target behavior, not shipped implementation. CONTRACT.md is
          unaccepted provenance and cannot add routes, signatures, defaults, errors, or proof
          requirements. Where Plans define no form, do not infer a signature from an export
          name or proposal inventory.
          CONTRACT.md §§7 and 9 remain provenance only. ARTIFACT_WIRE.md adds no schema.

        - Unaccepted §9 purposes and source amendments alone establish no signatures;
          owning Workflow Plans define the supported forms.
          - `graphOf`, `inspectTransition`, and `inspectMicrosteps`.
          - `inspectActivities`, `whyNoTransition`, and `formatNoTransitionSummary`.
          - `analyzeTrace`, `summarizeTrace`, `diffTrace`, and `formatTrace`.
          - `formatInspectionEvent`, `formatInspectionTimeline`, and
            `formatRehydrationSummary`.
          - `formatResourceFreshnessReport` and `formatTransactionOverlapSummary`.
          - `buildBehaviorContract`, `sliceBehaviorContract`, and `diffBehaviorContracts`.
          - `renderBehaviorContract`, `renderBehaviorCoverage`, and `renderBehaviorDiff`.

        | Proposal purpose | Status in unaccepted CONTRACT.md §9 |
        | --- | --- |
        | `attachInspectionSink` | One Runtime; returns `drain` and `dispose`; form unspecified. |
        | `createInspectionBufferSink` | Default 256; exposes `snapshot()` and `clear()`. |
        | `importTraceArtifact` | Accepts `Uint8Array`; private return type is unspecified. |
        | `exportTraceArtifact` | §9/AMEND-API-003 trace the signature at @plan.PublicSurface.O6. |
        | Compression helpers | Standalone purpose and full form remain unspecified. |

        - The compression names are `compressTraceArtifact` and `decompressTraceArtifact`.
verification
    V1  Persistence providers and declarations
        - Outcome: @O1.
        - Scenario:
          - Construct with and without a provider; exercise typed storage failures.
          - Test unsupported codec values, filters, browser adapters, and default-off declarations.
          - Test ordered/coalesced writes and disposal with an accepted write pending.
        - Pass:
          - Construction performs no I/O; provider, codec, and filter boundaries stay pure.
          - Runtime restores once before exposing handles.
          - Writes remain ordered, with the newest accepted value last.
          - Write failure preserves Runtime and the last record; it does not fail a committed turn.
          - Disposal stops observation and releases ownership.
        - Check: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types` and `nub run test`.
        - Target: add provider tests under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test`.
        - Pending: persistence provider behavior tests do not exist yet.

    V2  Durable boot, capture, hydration, and operation normalization
        - Outcome: @O2 and @O3.
        - Scenario:
          - Restore compatible and incompatible apps with stable and local refs.
          - Exercise provider/consumer graphs and concurrent store commits/captures.
          - Test opaque providers, resource bindings, streams, transactions, and missing executable
            P.
        - Pass:
          - Boot installs one canonical store and exact stable actors before activation.
          - Context baselines derive from restored providers.
          - Capture is atomic and context-closed or returns the specified typed failure.
          - Finite work and context events never replay.
          - Resource, stream, and transaction facts follow live-input rules.
        - Check: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types` and `nub run test`.
        - Existing proof: `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/readiness.test.ts`.
        - Target: add capture/hydration tests beside the readiness tests.
        - Pending: durable capture and hydration cases do not exist yet.

    V3  Codec bounds and failure ownership
        - Outcome: @O4.
        - Scenario:
          - Drive public byte paths with strict UTF-8, lone surrogates, JSON, and one gzip member.
          - Test duplicate keys where detectable, malformed values, and every envelope/key bound.
          - Test application-codec failures, bad identity/version, and raw parser errors.
        - Pass:
          - All surfaces share one decoder and the exact limits.
          - Input fails before mutation or work.
          - Failures preserve typed owner, category, path, and bound facts.
          - Parser exceptions, undefined, generic corruption, Cause trees, and Node Buffer do not
            leak.
        - Check: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types` and `nub run test`.
        - Target: add codec tests under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/artifact/tests`.
        - Pending: shared codec and bound tests do not exist yet.

    V4  Atomic evidence, sink, and truncation proof
        - Outcome: @O5.
        - Scenario:
          - Interleave publication, acknowledgments, StoreFanout, and late sink attachment.
          - Test clear, zero/default/custom capacity, accepted-prefix drains, and disposal.
          - Test slow/failing sinks and explicit export.
        - Pass:
          - Evidence stays globally ordered over committed cuts.
          - Sink work cannot delay or roll back truth.
          - Marker, clear, and late-attach semantics stay exact.
          - One sink attaches to one Runtime; skipped revision hints trigger rereads.
          - Only explicit export makes records durable.
        - Check: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types` and `nub run test`.
        - Existing proof:
          `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/evidence-sink-owner.test.ts`.
        - Pending: the target evidence path is incomplete.

    V5  Canonical v2 schemas, redaction, and Story evidence
        - Outcome: @O6 and @O7.
        - Scenario:
          - Encode/decode behavior and trace artifacts; compare fingerprints across AppPlans.
          - Reject malformed unions, nullable/member/order/identity errors.
          - Apply valid and invalid redactions.
          - Run direct and CLI Stories through checkpoints, end, cancellation, and cleanup.
          - Test pure-model discovery.
        - Pass:
          - Canonical bytes, models, and diagnostics match the contract.
          - Import rejects share kind before other validation.
          - Redaction changes only permitted values and the manifest.
          - Raw/share distinction and Story completion/failure/Cause remain exact.
          - Model discovery stays pure and shares one private model.
        - Check: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types` and `nub run test`.
        - Target: add artifact and Story tests under
          `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/artifact/tests`.
        - Pending: exact artifact schema and Story evidence tests do not exist yet.
